WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.745
A payment company logs every card authorization.

2
00:00:05.745 --> 00:00:10.820
The log is too big for one machine, so it is stored in blocks on three machines.

3
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:14.723
You want to count each status: approved, declined, refunded.

4
00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:20.011
Pulling the whole log onto one machine would push every byte through one network link.

5
00:00:20.011 --> 00:00:23.085
So each machine works on the block it already has.

6
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.049
First, map.

7
00:00:24.049 --> 00:00:30.311
Each machine reads its block and turns every word into a pair: the word, and the number one.

8
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:32.002
That makes nine pairs.

9
00:00:32.002 --> 00:00:34.337
Now, which machine counts approved?

10
00:00:34.337 --> 00:00:36.740
Three reducers will do the counting.

11
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:41.825
Each machine hashes the key and takes the remainder after dividing by three.

12
00:00:41.825 --> 00:00:43.743
That number picks the reducer.

13
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:50.032
The same key gives the same number on every machine, so approved always goes to reducer one.

14
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:51.310
Now the shuffle.

15
00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.407
Every pair crosses the network to its reducer.

16
00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:57.098
Each reducer sorts what it got by key.

17
00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:02.092
Reducer one now holds all five approved pairs, from three different machines.

18
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.554
Reduce adds them up: approved five, declined two, refunded two.

19
00:01:06.554 --> 00:01:11.551
No reducer ever needs to ask another, because each key lives in one place.

20
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:14.173
Look at what crossed the network, though.

21
00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:16.815
The same pair, approved one, again and again.

22
00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:21.442
A combiner runs the reduce step on each machine, before the shuffle.

23
00:01:21.442 --> 00:01:25.591
Node A turns its two approved ones into one pair: approved, two.

24
00:01:25.591 --> 00:01:26.952
Node B does the same.

25
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:33.040
Now seven pairs cross the network instead of nine, and the reducers still get the same answer.

26
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:36.416
On a real log, a map task reads a million events.

27
00:01:36.416 --> 00:01:41.301
With a combiner, it sends three pairs, one per status, instead of a million.

28
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:43.518
Map runs where the data lives.

29
00:01:43.518 --> 00:01:46.560
A hash sends every key to exactly one reducer.

30
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.065
And a combiner shrinks what has to cross the network.
